#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097629} :activity/id #uuid "3dec8fd5-96f5-5d03-a211-5d2ff3da55fc" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050155 :topic/id #uuid "3274c28d-4be4-51fc-9211-e22afa89f69d" :topic/math-academy-id 1088} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097629 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097801 :tutorial/content "Suppose that the sequence $a_n$ is defined as\n\n$$\na_n =  3\\left(\\dfrac 1 2\\right)^n, \\qquad n\\geq 1.\n$$\n\nThis is a geometric sequence with common ratio $r = \\dfrac12.$ What is the behavior of this sequence as $n\\to\\infty?$\n\nLet's start by calculating the first few terms of the sequence:\n\n$$\na_1 = \\dfrac32, \\qquad a_2 = \\dfrac34, \\qquad a_3 = \\dfrac38, \\qquad a_4 = \\dfrac{3}{16}, \\qquad \\ldots\\,.\n$$\n\nPlotting the few terms of the sequence, we get a graph like the one below:\n\n![](images/8b/8b31b82126b56226a74aac5f3406d2d4ad5c85ec5c468e089acd028eba6c0469.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, it appears that $a_n\\to 0$ as $n\\to\\infty.$ This is indeed true.\n\nBecause $a_n$ approaches zero as $n\\to \\infty,$ we say that the sequence is **convergent** and that it **converges to **$0.$ We also say that the **limit of the sequence** is $0.$\n\nThe sequence is convergent because $|r| < 1,$ which causes the terms of the sequence to decrease to zero as $n$ increases." :tutorial/id #uuid "c977d786-2e9c-5b46-9aae-5025af2b3da6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1149 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e2fca69c-5a2d-5ac6-a30b-3592861d6d4e" :step/math-academy-id 2262 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097674}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097630 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096744 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096551 :question/id #uuid "bc0f8f02-ba1c-59c7-ba27-cb2148f2a9f8" :question/math-academy-id "e-646"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4380b34-0502-552b-b94f-e121eb855f1d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048829}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097236} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234113} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442201} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442210} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554583} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554596}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Convergence Behavior of Geometric Sequences Written Recursively"} :step/id #uuid "bd6fc951-0dda-51a1-aeb0-9e73b5e47429" :step/math-academy-id 2263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097674 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097879 :tutorial/content "Now let's look at the sequence $b_n,$ defined as\n\n$$\nb_n =  2^n, \\qquad n\\geq 1.\n$$\n\nThis is a geometric sequence with common ratio $r = 2.$ What is the behavior of this sequence as $n\\to\\infty?$\n\nLet's start by calculating the first few terms of the sequence:\n\n$$\nb_1 = 2, \\qquad b_2 = 4, \\qquad b_3 = 8, \\qquad b_4 = 16, \\qquad \\ldots\\,.\n$$\n\nPlotting the few terms of the sequence, we get a graph like the one below:\n\n![](images/62/629ac7251857a5c79d0648e3d68d13ebfce57d560c43af1c9662636d04b5702f.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, it appears that $b_n\\to \\infty$ as $n\\to\\infty.$ This is indeed true.\n\nBecause $b_n$ grows without bound as $n\\to \\infty,$ we say that the sequence is **divergent.** The sequence is divergent because $|r| > 1,$ which causes the terms of the sequence to increase as $n$ increases." :tutorial/id #uuid "63956297-e5ed-5ed5-9fd1-21167efbfab9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4865 :tutorial/title "Divergent Geometric Sequences"} :step/id #uuid "0590acbf-3f08-519e-8730-b102037d3717" :step/math-academy-id 30462 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097715}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097675 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097880 :tutorial/content "To summarize, suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term $a_1$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r| <    1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r| > 1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $a_1$\n\n- if $r = -1,$ the sequence is divergent" :tutorial/id #uuid "545dc12e-bce3-5e0f-bc1e-62d444e9ba7a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4866 :tutorial/title "A Summary of Convergence Results For Geometric Sequences"} :step/id #uuid "e5664d35-ec55-5895-8884-65c6fdf62973" :step/math-academy-id 30464 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097710}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097710 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096795 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096604 :question/id #uuid "90ed1cce-a831-58b0-942a-d8b9a17583cc" :question/math-academy-id "e-9964"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a850ba4f-dbb5-5a09-afb0-3dc3db709420" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049688} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050255} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050977}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097109} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097110} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442205} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442206} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442208}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Geometric Sequence Converges"} :step/id #uuid "b420bd1a-a7ba-53a6-948c-f09c441c35f4" :step/math-academy-id 37514 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097711}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097711 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096796 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096605 :question/id #uuid "0bbb71a2-ff71-5f25-80bc-e56bbed9ac1f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9989"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5986f765-9036-5124-a498-bd898119c9c5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050067}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097281} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097326} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442200} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442203} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442204} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554567} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554601}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Geometric Sequence Converges Given the Nth Term"} :step/id #uuid "b7aaa5b4-33fb-5c5f-a0de-7d674061edc6" :step/math-academy-id 37628 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097630}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097715 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097900 :tutorial/content "Okay, so we've seen that a geometric sequence is convergent for $|r| < 1$ and divergent when $|r| >1.$ But what about when $|r| = 1?$\n\nWe have two cases:\n\n- When $r=1,$ the geometric sequence is convergent and converges to the first term. \n\nFor example, consider the sequence $c_n = 2(1)^n,$ shown below:\n\n![](images/6f/6f11903fd259e75a51de9877fadbb97e975b48bce229bad954aae2d6b3da36c9.png)\n\nIn this case, the sequence is convergent, and the limit of the sequence is $c_1 = 2.$\n\n- When $r=-1,$ the geometric sequence is divergent. \n\nFor example, consider the sequence $d_n = 2(-1)^n,$ shown below:\n\n![](images/3e/3e4de425d4e6af816380b275e536685f524adfb33643715b093d6def3762f03a.png)\n\nIn this case, the sequence oscillates between $2$ and $-2.$ It does not approach a fixed number, so it is divergent." :tutorial/id #uuid "1c43e3ce-7fc5-51e4-91e8-d598ab3fb1f2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6223 :tutorial/title "Edge Cases"} :step/id #uuid "80e4b31c-2592-53cf-b150-6650033614f3" :step/math-academy-id 37798 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097675}}] :activity/title "Convergence of Geometric Sequences" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096877}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554662} :activity/id #uuid "5a54a8e0-e20e-5fbb-9abb-7d3113964568" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050155 :topic/id #uuid "3274c28d-4be4-51fc-9211-e22afa89f69d" :topic/math-academy-id 1088} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554647 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554567 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e72f358-8b3c-5fa0-a87f-b5c3e703b4d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The sequence diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553923} {:answer/id #uuid "22b7211e-dd6c-5a3e-88e8-d3edd2915633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Converges to $0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553929} {:answer/id #uuid "6d1afbe3-f463-50af-b060-99529ce98d35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Converges to $\\frac{2}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554083} {:answer/id #uuid "909b2006-2b10-5c79-9d1c-af317380a62c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Converges to $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554182} {:answer/id #uuid "9c753113-557c-5b1c-8957-36be50789e48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Converges to $\\frac{4}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554212}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553929} :answer-field/id #uuid "5521173e-4fd0-569c-b113-b91da149682d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554462}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "41a5c3bd-58e0-5924-89f4-127ab078d31e" :question/math-academy-id "q-141408" :question/problem "Does the sequence ${a}_{n}=2{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n}$ for $n\\ge 1$ \nconverge or diverge? If it converges, what is its limit?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nThe given sequence ${a}_{n}=2{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n}$ is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r=\\frac{2}{3}.$ \n\nSince $|r|<1,$ the sequence converges to $0.$"} :step/id #uuid "0c2f224c-fc34-5e4b-af33-9729624ea81e"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554662 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097236 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4b751e1-2690-566a-8d59-1bf7bcdc09c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Converges to $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095341} {:answer/id #uuid "bf4bd6a7-89b1-58be-92f1-e02d983de0c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Converges to $\\frac{2}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095342} {:answer/id #uuid "fe9db267-0789-594f-b053-f9a128344946" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The sequence diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095343} {:answer/id #uuid "136f2048-5a19-5273-b2d9-93d3126c405d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Converges to $0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095344} {:answer/id #uuid "7ff7d36a-2462-5f79-9079-fd54b8c9a26e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Converges to $-\\frac{2}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095345}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186095344} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ccc59c6-df91-53a0-b02a-f0cfa5848876" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095340}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b6bc5be2-67de-545d-b0ff-52b4105c5d45" :question/math-academy-id "q-40234" :question/problem "Consider the following geometric sequence:\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{2}{3}{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=5,\\,n\\ge 1\n$$\n\nDetermine whether the sequence is convergent or divergent. If it converges, find the limit." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nThe given sequence is a geometric sequence with the common ratio \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nSince $|r|=\\frac{2}{3}<1$, the sequence converges to $0.$"} :step/id #uuid "35276136-1141-5a0d-bfff-d48f2981934d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554702}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554670 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554601 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56ac1be1-fce4-5b14-a773-c0df14c2a9b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Converges to $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554039} {:answer/id #uuid "6417f28a-8f7f-5ecc-be6c-7c8da2415e24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Converges to $\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554064} {:answer/id #uuid "8301edff-e58a-593a-be70-3f68ef33e5f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The sequence diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554133} {:answer/id #uuid "8435d0e8-40cd-5cd4-89d6-083368872674" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Converges to $0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554139} {:answer/id #uuid "8f866f3c-7689-5311-b7e1-0b559c9e9370" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Converges to $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554176}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554139} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbf8a36f-7f35-538f-b598-e5154c361dd2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554523}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8f0ee2b9-8191-5f49-b4c7-7195eb067ecb" :question/math-academy-id "q-141404" :question/problem "Does the sequence ${a}_{n}=4{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n-1}$ for $n\\ge 1$ \nconverge or diverge? If it converges, what is its limit?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nThe given sequence ${a}_{n}=4{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n-1}$ is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r=\\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nSince $|r|<1,$ the sequence converges to $0.$"} :step/id #uuid "41bf86ad-c7cd-5a20-938c-161c8506ba22" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554647}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554702 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554583 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43955b41-f6e8-5349-9111-56dd4e7126c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Converges to $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553994} {:answer/id #uuid "6cdc885e-37b4-5a68-a0b9-c54691be5651" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Converges to $0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554082} {:answer/id #uuid "a6fb9534-7b00-51a6-9b6b-3e0661951505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The sequence diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554238} {:answer/id #uuid "ba63283c-6ba0-5b52-bd92-e677158a64ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Converges to $\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554273} {:answer/id #uuid "f4d729f2-8517-5f08-b3f8-1bda0315fedc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Converges to $-4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554399}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554238} :answer-field/id #uuid "4eef3b91-cbf1-5165-b4b4-6db1ea5e763f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554458}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6b244d67-f2e6-5997-9db2-2abb0586d4be" :question/math-academy-id "q-123333" :question/problem "Consider the following geometric sequence:\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=2{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=-4,\\,n\\ge 1\n$$\n\nDetermine whether the sequence is convergent or divergent. If it converges, find the limit." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nThe given sequence is a geometric sequence with the common ratio \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=2.\n$$\n\nSince $|r|=2>1$, the sequence diverges."} :step/id #uuid "8bc84e8c-018f-5eec-8644-703daf8e1b21" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554722}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554722 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554596 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d1a1119-11c9-535c-a456-e45cc9d7de82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Converges to $0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553896} {:answer/id #uuid "55d44151-35f1-54c2-9d4d-cb890968345b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Converges to $-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554035} {:answer/id #uuid "c9f1522c-e116-5be3-a536-f1ce7ed8e514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Converges to $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554313} {:answer/id #uuid "e0c72dcf-0cfe-526c-b0ed-899e73f67e02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The sequence diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554356} {:answer/id #uuid "f0d2babb-cf96-5a12-b155-7886cd64321e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Converges to $-\\frac{1}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554391}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554356} :answer-field/id #uuid "77095092-5c7c-58b9-9d24-8f2368d8a83f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554479}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8875866b-8dd0-5b03-ad00-243a32baa5f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-123334" :question/problem "Consider the following geometric sequence:\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=4{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{4},\\,n\\ge 1\n$$\n\nDetermine whether the sequence is convergent or divergent. If it converges, find the limit." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nThe given sequence is a geometric sequence with the common ratio \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=4.\n$$\n\nSince $|r|=4>1$, the sequence diverges."} :step/id #uuid "af316307-fe77-5742-bcd8-358325e5d827" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554670}}] :activity/title "Convergence of Geometric Sequences" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553846}]}
